.Dd October 7, 2026
.Dt GOLDIN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm goldin
.Nd Divide a two-fork file into a data fork and an AppleDouble encoded
resource fork
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl h , Fl \-help , Fl u
.Op Fl a , Fl \-apfs
.Op Fl d , Fl \-dryrun
.Op Fl l , Fl \-linksplit
.Op Fl s , Fl \-strip
.Op Fl v , Fl \-verbose
.Op Fl w , Fl \-warnsymlink
.Op Fl \-norecurse
.Op Fl \-version
.Ar PATH ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Goldin does the same job as
.Xr SplitForks 1
with some enhanced functionality.
It converts Macintosh HFS or HFS Extended ("HFS+") two-fork files into
AppleDouble format with the data fork and the resource fork plus file system
metadata in separate files.
The original SplitForks only acted on one file.
Goldin accepts one or more paths, and will search any directory passed to it
recursively for files to convert, unless
.Fl \-norecurse
is given.
.Pp
Each
.Ar PATH
is split itself as well as its contents, so a folder with Finder information
gets its own AppleDouble file alongside it, in its parent folder.
.Pp
Symbolic links inside a folder are neither split nor followed.
A
.Ar PATH
that is a symbolic link is resolved first: its target is split, and the
target's AppleDouble file goes alongside the target.
With
.Fl w ,
goldin reports each item it splits this way.
Files with more than one hard link are not split unless
.Fl l
is given.
Goldin doesn't descend into volumes mounted inside a folder.
The root of a volume can't be split itself, because its AppleDouble file
would have to go in a folder on another volume.
If a
.Ar PATH
is the root of a volume and has Finder information, goldin reports an error;
otherwise its contents are split as usual.
.Pp
Paths are processed in turn.
Folders that cannot be read, including a
.Ar PATH
itself, are reported and skipped, and processing continues.
Any other error is reported and stops goldin without processing the
remaining paths.
.Pp
Like SplitForks, goldin only works on HFS and HFS+ volumes unless
.Fl a
is given, which allows APFS volumes as well.
These file systems store resource forks and Finder information natively.
Other file systems are always refused, because on those macOS keeps a file's
resource fork and Finder information in its own
.Ql \&._
file, which the AppleDouble file goldin writes would collide with.
.Pp
.Nm
accepts these options and arguments:
.Bl -tag -width -indent
.It Fl h , Fl \-help , Fl u
Print usage information and exit.
.Fl u
is kept for backwards compatibility.
.It Fl a , Fl \-apfs
Allow APFS volumes as well as HFS and HFS+.
.It Fl d , Fl \-dryrun
Check what goldin would do, without writing anything.
Goldin lists every file and folder it would split, with their paths shown
from where the path they all share ends, which the first line gives.
Below an item, each problem goldin finds with it is shown on a line of its
own, indented by four spaces and beginning
.Ql WARNING:\& .
Problems that would stop goldin end with
.Ql goldin stops here ;
the dry run carries on, so that every problem is listed.
Goldin checks, without writing, that it can read the item's Finder
information and resource fork, that the AppleDouble file's name and path are
not too long, that the folder it goes in can be written to and is not locked,
that any existing AppleDouble file can be replaced, and that the volume has
room for all the AppleDouble files.
Folders that can't be read are listed with a warning, as are
.Ar PATH Ns s
that can't be processed.
With
.Fl v ,
goldin lists every item, including those it would not split, beginning
each line with
.Ql Process:\&
or
.Ql Ignored:\& ,
and giving the reason an item would be ignored in parentheses.
With
.Fl w ,
items reached through a symbolic link get a warning.
.Fl s
is ignored.
.It Fl l , Fl \-linksplit
Split files with more than one hard link as well.
Each name of the file gets its own AppleDouble file.
With
.Fl s ,
these files are split but their resource fork is not stripped: stripping it
through one name would remove it from the file under all its names, and those
elsewhere may have no AppleDouble file.
.It Fl s , Fl \-strip
Strip the resource fork from the original file.
The default is to leave the resource fork in place after copying it to its
AppleDouble metadata file.
.It Fl v , Fl \-verbose
Verbose mode.
Prints to standard output when a file is to be split.
.It Fl w , Fl \-warnsymlink
Report, on standard error, each item that is split, or split and stripped,
after being reached through a symbolic link in a
.Ar PATH
as it was given: a
.Ar PATH
that is a symbolic link, or that goes through one.
The report names the symbolic link.
It does not change the exit status.
.It Fl \-norecurse
Only split the files and folders directly inside each
.Ar PATH ;
don't descend into its subdirectories.
.It Fl \-version
Print the tool's version and exit.
.It Ar PATH ...
One or more files or directories to split.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width -indent
.It Pa /usr/local/bin/goldin
The installed tool.
.It example
A file to convert.
.It example.d/
A directory to search for files to convert.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success.
With
.Fl d ,
no problems were found.
.It 1
With
.Fl d ,
problems were found.
.It 2
An unknown option was given.
.It 254
A
.Ar PATH
is not on an HFS or HFS+ volume, or with
.Fl a ,
not on an HFS, HFS+ or APFS volume.
.It 255
Any other error, including no
.Ar PATH
being given.
.El
.Sh NOTES
Goldin will fail with an error if a file or directory is not on an HFS or
HFS+ volume, or with
.Fl a ,
not on an HFS, HFS+ or APFS volume.
.Pp
Unless goldin is run as root, the AppleDouble files it creates are owned by
the user running it.
Only the permissions of the original file are copied, not its owner or group.
.Pp
Files that macOS has compressed keep their compressed data in a hidden
resource fork.
That is not a real resource fork, and goldin leaves it alone.
.Pp
Goldin lives here:
.Lk https://github.com/packagesdev/goldin .
.Pp
To know why this tool is named goldin, see
.Lk https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sawing_a_woman_in_half
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr FixupResourceForks 1 ,
.Xr goldout 1 ,
.Xr SplitForks 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 1740 documents AppleSingle/AppleDouble formats.